# Installation

{{ display_name }} publishes the same skills to four runtimes: Claude Code, Codex, OpenCode and Pi. Pick your runtime below. The skills are generated into `skills/` and the plugin manifests at the repository root.

| Runtime | Route |
| --- | --- |
| [Claude Code](#claude-code) | Plugin marketplace |
| [Codex](#codex) | Local plugin marketplace from a clone |
| [OpenCode](#opencode) | Git only: point OpenCode at the skills |
| [Pi](#pi) | Pi package from {{ 'npm or git' if publish_to_npm else 'git' }} |

## Claude Code

```sh
claude plugin marketplace add {{ github_owner }}/{{ repo_name }}
claude plugin install {{ repo_name }}@{{ repo_name }}
```

Inside a session the same steps are `/plugin marketplace add {{ github_owner }}/{{ repo_name }}` and `/plugin install {{ repo_name }}@{{ repo_name }}`. Update with `claude plugin marketplace update {{ repo_name }}`, then `claude plugin update {{ repo_name }}@{{ repo_name }}`. Run `/reload-plugins` or start a new session to load the skills.

## Codex

Clone the repository, then register the clone as a local marketplace:

```sh
git clone {{ repo_url }}
codex plugin marketplace add /absolute/path/to/{{ repo_name }}
codex plugin add {{ repo_name }}@{{ repo_name }}
```

Start a new thread to load the skills. To update, pull the clone and run `codex plugin add {{ repo_name }}@{{ repo_name }}` again.

## OpenCode

OpenCode installs from git only. Clone the repository, then make each skill discoverable. OpenCode searches `.agents/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` in the project and `~/.agents/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` globally, so link every folder under `skills/` into one of those:

```sh
git clone {{ repo_url }}
mkdir -p ~/.agents/skills
for skill in {{ repo_name }}/skills/*/; do ln -s "$PWD/${skill%/}" ~/.agents/skills/; done
```

Alternatively, add the clone's `skills/` directory to OpenCode's configuration:

```json
{
  "$schema": "https://opencode.ai/config.json",
  "skills": {
    "paths": ["/absolute/path/to/{{ repo_name }}/skills"]
  }
}
```

To update, run `git pull` in the clone; nothing was copied.

> **Open question.** The official OpenCode documentation shows no way for an npm package to ship skills, so there is no npm route for OpenCode. If that changes, add it here.

## Pi

{% if publish_to_npm -%}
```sh
pi install npm:{{ repo_name }}
# or from git
pi install git:github.com/{{ github_owner }}/{{ repo_name }}
```
{%- else -%}
This package is not published to npm, so install it from git:

```sh
pi install git:github.com/{{ github_owner }}/{{ repo_name }}
```
{%- endif %}

The package declares the `pi-package` keyword and a `pi.skills` entry pointing at `./skills` in `package.json`. Restart Pi or reload resources after installing or updating.
